    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1033f8c2_f424de80",
        "filename": "sdk/update.go",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 365,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-05T20:27:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok. It is not clear from the documentation of rule builder that it has advantages over other approaches, it simply states that it is an alternative approach. Some information about its advantages and disadvantages and when to use the different forms would be useful.\n\nIn this particular case are there any advantages to using rule builder instead of Rule that you can see.\n\nWhat do you mean by \u0027implicit dependencies\u0027? Do you have a concrete example?\n\nIn terms of improving RuleBuilder I did have a look but was not sure how best to approach it. Ideally, what I would like is to be able to extract information by name from the BuildParams. I need to be careful because given the size of the generated ninja file (1.8GB) adding extra information to BuildParams could significantly increase the memory footprint. So, extending it to add more information seems like it is not a viable approach. So, the information needs to be encoded in the existing structure, e.g. the Args field.\n\nAs I see it there are two approaches:\n1) Add copies of existing methods, e.g. Input(Path), Flag(string), that take an extra parameter that is the name of the option which gets stored in the BuildParams Args field, e.g. NamedInput(string, Path), NamedFlag(string, string).\n\n2) Add a modifier method that causes the following method to be treated as a named method. e.g. something like NamedArg(\u003cname\u003e).Input(Path) which causes the input path to be stored in the build params Args field.\n\nAIUI they would both have to generate $\u003cname\u003e in the command line leaving ninja to do the substitution.\n\nThe former would essentially double the number of methods (although I would not need to add them all up front). The latter would be complicated both in the implementation and use.\n\nBefore I invest a not insignificant amount of time on this I was wondering what you thought would be a good approach.",
      "parentUuid": "dca4ae4d_79322922",
      "revId": "c56bd709d43549ccb849b24d162efef227367353",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c56ab498_73ce1a6d",
        "filename": "sdk/update.go",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 365,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-05T21:03:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For example in the current rules you have to separately track Command vs. CommandDeps, and it\u0027s easy to add a new tool to Command without adding a dependency on it in CommandDeps.  In RuleBuilder it\u0027s just RuleBuilderCommand.BuildTool(), and the dependency is handled automatically.\n\nA few thoughts:\nDon\u0027t worry about increasing the size of BuildParams, it is normally thrown away after calling Build() unless running in a test (m.config.captureBuild is set).\n\nYou don\u0027t have to get the values from the BuildParams at all, you can easily stash the values on the *sdk itself to access via ctx.ModuleForTests(...).Module().(*sdk).\n\nI don\u0027t have a problem with the NamedArg() approach, although it will be tricky to get right.  It should probably return a new object that captures values as long as it is used, and not try to match the name with the next call to set a value.\n\nDepending on the type of value you want to check it would also be possible to capture the RuleBuilder when m.config.captureBuild is set, and then add methods to query the values that were passed to specific flags.  For example:\n\n cmd.FlagWithInput(\"-i \", input)\n\nand then in the test:\n\n ctx.ModuleForTests(...).RuleBuilderRule(\"zip\").FlagValue(\"-i \")",
      "parentUuid": "1033f8c2_f424de80",
      "revId": "c56bd709d43549ccb849b24d162efef227367353",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
